menu "app_httpd -- device web UI"

config APP_HTTPD_ENABLE
    bool "Serve the device status page and provisioning portal"
    default y
    help
        This is an unauthenticated control surface: anyone on the LAN can reboot the
        board or make it check for an update. Acceptable on a lab network, not on a
        shared one. Turn it off, or put a shared secret in front of it, before anything
        ships (PLAN §7).

config APP_HTTPD_PORT
    int "HTTP port"
    default 80
    depends on APP_HTTPD_ENABLE

config APP_HTTPD_MAX_SOCKETS
    int "Maximum simultaneous connections"
    default 4
    range 1 12
    depends on APP_HTTPD_ENABLE
    help
        Kept low on purpose. Per-connection server buffers sit alongside mbedTLS's
        during an OTA download, and on a low-RAM part that is precisely when both are
        largest (PLAN §7). Test the update path with the page open in a browser.

config APP_HTTPD_STACK
    int "HTTP server task stack size"
    default 5120
    depends on APP_HTTPD_ENABLE

config APP_HTTPD_CAPTIVE_PORTAL
    bool "Answer captive-portal probes and hijack DNS while the AP is up"
    default y
    depends on APP_HTTPD_ENABLE
    help
        Right, and the login sheet pops by itself when a phone joins the provisioning
        AP. Wrong, and the user has to somehow know to browse to 192.168.4.1. The DNS
        responder binds only to the AP interface and only while the AP is up, so it
        never answers for clients on your real network.

config APP_HTTPD_SCAN_MAX
    int "Maximum networks listed by the portal"
    default 20
    range 4 40
    depends on APP_HTTPD_ENABLE

config APP_HTTPD_COREDUMP_DOWNLOAD
    bool "Allow downloading the raw crash dump over HTTP"
    default n
    depends on APP_HTTPD_ENABLE
    help
        The status page always *reports* a stored crash dump -- which task, which program
        counter, the panic reason. That is diagnosis, and it gives nothing away.

        This option additionally serves the raw dump at GET /api/coredump.bin so it can be
        fed to esp-coredump on a workstation. A core dump is a copy of task stacks, so a
        crash that happened during Wi-Fi work can contain the network passphrase, and this
        server has no authentication at all (PLAN §4a). Default off, and it should stay off
        anywhere the LAN is not yours.

        With it off, a dump is still recoverable over a serial cable:
            esptool read-flash <coredump offset> <size> dump.bin

endmenu
